#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfc0d9e1-bce1-5d62-b3c8-f7d7bb226497" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1e039d-033d-5fb2-afe2-058a41532346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=[-1,1]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399386} {:answer/id #uuid "b5518e7e-ad19-5fdf-b048-f6f07aa85005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399387} {:answer/id #uuid "a777899f-4fb2-599e-8bac-fd1080f27455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399388} {:answer/id #uuid "730ca820-682a-52b2-87ed-4d379934e772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399389} {:answer/id #uuid "89a3a902-db89-527e-ac98-aebc7e8b5c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a777899f-4fb2-599e-8bac-fd1080f27455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399388} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3aed52a-be07-5913-ae72-4601a0fc9425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c3c2591-89b8-5e94-a7b0-e02f5b424693" :question/math-academy-id "q-103125" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+5)^{n+1}}{(n+1)(n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{(x+5)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{n(x+5)}{n+2}| \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+2} \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x+5|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+5|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+5| & <1 \\\\ -1<x+5 & <1 \\\\ -6<x & <-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-6,-4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x+5|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-6$ and $x=-4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=-6$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-6+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=-4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},$\nwe see that the series is convergent by the comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=[-6,-4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce17c65-7d67-54af-bb8e-a4b548957fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500495} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc573f9-6cba-59f5-957c-6c9a34a77f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500566} {:answer/id #uuid "62f66835-664a-578e-9ff4-452e7799954b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=r+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500673} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c7097-61f8-5853-b475-d4c6e09f84d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500728} {:answer/id #uuid "f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500992} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e2c35a2-00a1-5395-9ca6-d665e10ade6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8d1bea5-b961-5d22-9bfd-ba62f26db131" :question/math-academy-id "q-119161" :question/problem "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the *square of its radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kr}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb529d81-61f3-5c3a-9ee9-1db6044fbb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461965} {:answer/id #uuid "7d60155f-814b-5688-b80c-ff69952ec3fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb529d81-61f3-5c3a-9ee9-1db6044fbb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461965} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4d0f6ff-d5d7-5577-b526-af22d3855f19" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6edd1c4-8271-5dbf-a1a6-4d911168312f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461967} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d84be9-03de-5a03-987f-f1a67f85e17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6edd1c4-8271-5dbf-a1a6-4d911168312f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461967} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3087f9-0cbe-58bf-bce0-511b72140cf0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1637f773-701a-5c85-a0b6-4ffc31761b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461969} {:answer/id #uuid "490949a0-6a54-5bc2-b39c-42807015af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "490949a0-6a54-5bc2-b39c-42807015af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461970} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d67781-1cc9-577e-b688-09b5d2ad50e6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c58940-535c-5bff-aff2-b382e99c5568" :question/math-academy-id "q-120282" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e90d9a160db2c75091253eaa8a8c815e92c45f988841683f5a4d18ca6a6ea7db.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/54/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083341 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cab29074-3281-5c02-9a2b-423077675aef" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Test in Divergent and Inconclusive Cases"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16eb9f4b-4d3a-531a-a851-09494af3ba39" :question/math-academy-id "q-138998" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8813b8a0-3b5e-5cd8-a37d-6b921f2882b8" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as the Sum of a Number and a Geometric Series"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b297bef-d86e-59df-8ce3-4a3826383e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-40691" :question/problem "$2.3\\overset{―}{43}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.343\\,43... \\\\  & =2.3+0.043+0.000\\,43+0.000\\,004\\,3+… \\\\  & =2.3+{{\\frac{43}{1\\,000}+\\frac{43}{100\\,000}+\\frac{43}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{43}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43\\cdot 100}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c034fbc0-b497-53f2-8e9c-9d12afa04ec2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Series Whose Denominator Is the Product of Two Consecutive Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54e664a-f1d7-5bcb-8e94-1a6ce5022d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099910} {:answer/id #uuid "100c1476-f281-54e0-95a7-7818ad4fe29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099911} {:answer/id #uuid "12064d86-564b-5b56-8f66-38d0279ac64d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099912} {:answer/id #uuid "35745ab3-9c05-512d-aeca-22c906dffd62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099913} {:answer/id #uuid "43bdaffc-0049-5e1e-bbe6-abbbc04ef425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099914}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fac9b251-da24-5c85-a8c5-19a64644ffc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099909}] :question/id #uuid "a76f098c-1049-59cd-81d6-41ec2e42a5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49685" :question/problem "Calculate\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2} + 5n + 6}$.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d71bc2-c0f0-5504-9f79-a5f5cdb8cd92" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator Contains a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48658c86-5fff-5d1e-ad97-ca9615485d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339773} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c23eb-6ff2-5bd1-ab4a-f009409818c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340399} {:answer/id #uuid "a839708e-6d86-5a57-b693-6f0e21e650eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340655} {:answer/id #uuid "ced86a89-239e-50b2-894e-628af504a0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340966} {:answer/id #uuid "daa4acb0-2d88-582f-a718-be7e56ba1265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa4acb0-2d88-582f-a718-be7e56ba1265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341071} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2bfd72-4857-53aa-b6a7-f5d94cbdaa7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40d2e0b8-4256-5b72-ae20-79f2c7d72c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-73391" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02b30695-cbca-5de7-a2d0-90b3f98db6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-75637" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $56$ and a third term equal to $\\frac{7}{8}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=56$ and ${a}_{3}=\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{8})}{56} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =(\\frac{7}{8})(\\frac{1}{56}) \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(\\frac{7}{8})} \\\\  & =56(\\frac{8}{7}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5180e91d-b92b-505b-ba26-cfc2c02ae8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(2{e}^{2\\pi }+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349187} {:answer/id #uuid "59b0c393-ff53-5296-86d7-57e19a153481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349273} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfce7d1-3045-5f79-9f67-af6e541e7d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2\\pi }+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349724} {:answer/id #uuid "a1611af0-6b4b-511c-bdc0-8ccfe63b5718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349880} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f0ee9b-6deb-5faa-8e4b-9fac96efdbec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1611af0-6b4b-511c-bdc0-8ccfe63b5718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2580160-8816-5519-b6e4-1d9a9bef341c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbc072d0-2ec3-57b6-ba23-0219f96efe5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83012" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
